    {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "The **published patch-clamp result should drive the initial safety decision**, provided it used cisapride itself and a credible hERG assay.\n\nWhy:\n\n- Patch clamp measures **functional inhibition of hERG current**, the endpoint relevant to QT prolongation and arrhythmia risk.\n- Radioligand displacement measures **binding**, which may not track functional block because of channel state, membrane environment, assay temperature, protocol, or ligand-specific effects.\n- A weak binding result therefore does not rule out potent state-dependent or functionally effective channel block.\n\nDo not ignore the screen: reconcile assay conditions, concentration ranges, channel construct, temperature, and exposure. Compare the patch-clamp IC50 with **free clinical Cmax**. If block occurs near relevant exposure, treat cisapride as a hERG liability and use that result for go/no-go decisions. If the patch-clamp effect occurs only far above exposure or has questionable controls, repeat a standardized functional assay rather than averaging the two results.",
